@startuml 0028-boot-env-snapshot-ordering
!pragma layout smetana
title ADR-0028 — why the snapshot must be the entry point's first import

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left

participant "node\n(module evaluation)" as Node
participant "config/env-snapshot.ts" as Snap
participant "app.module.ts\n→ PrismaService" as App
participant "@prisma/client" as Prisma
participant "process.env" as Env
participant "bootstrap()" as Boot

== Module evaluation — imports run in source order ==

Node -> Snap : import 1 (deliberately ahead of alphabetical order)
activate Snap
Snap -> Env : read presence of the 7 guarded names
Env --> Snap : the operator's own configuration
note right of Snap
  PRESENT_BEFORE_PRISMA_COULD_LOAD
  frozen here, once
end note
deactivate Snap

Node -> App : import 2 (would sort first)
activate App
App -> Prisma : reached at import time via PrismaService
activate Prisma
Prisma -> Env : merge schema-adjacent apps/api/.env\n(import side effect, fill-only)
note right of Env
  Whatever the operator left unset
  is now silently supplied by a file.
end note
deactivate Prisma
deactivate App

== bootstrap() — after everything above has already run ==

Node -> Boot
activate Boot
Boot -> Env : read live environment
Env --> Boot
Boot -> Boot : names present now\nminus names present before
alt NODE_ENV=production and something arrived
  Boot -->o] : refuse to start —\nname the variables and the file,\nnever the values
else otherwise
  Boot -> Boot : no-op (F6: never reject a working configuration)
end
deactivate Boot

legend right
  Swap imports 1 and 2 and the snapshot is taken
  AFTER the merge: nothing looks like it arrived,
  the guard goes blind, and the process runs on
  values nobody set.
  That is the control proof (ADR-0021) the
  acceptance suite runs as its own case.
endlegend

@enduml
